## Verdict on the guess

My guess was wrong on the specific mechanism (no live multi-byte panic), but the read surfaced a latent slice-index fragility at lines 1237/1244: the safety of `lines[..AUTO_CONTEXT_MAX_LINES] silently depends on the invariant AUTO_CONTEXT_LARGE_FILE >= AUTO_CONTEXT_MAX_LINES`. These are two independently-tunable constants three lines apart. A future edit lowering LARGE_FILE below `MAX_LINES` turns line 1237 into an out-of-bounds panic on any file whose line count lands in the gap.

Per the Day-137 lesson, a pinning test is justified only when there's documented temptation. Here the temptation is concrete and structural: two adjacent tunable knobs whose relationship is an unstated invariant that silently underpins an unchecked slice. That is exactly the "co-located tunable invariant" temptation. 

But rather than just pin the invariant with a test (which could still be violated by a future edit that also updates the test), the **surgical, correct fix** is to make the slice itself panic-proof regardless of constant values — use `.min(total_lines)` so the code can never go out of bounds. That removes the invariant dependency entirely. I'll write a failing test first that exercises the fragility, then apply the fix.

